<p>None of these has good evidence that it stops hair fall or treats pattern hair loss. Rosemary oil has one small 2015 trial in which it performed similarly to a low-strength minoxidil, onion juice has one small study in a different condition, and oils mainly protect the hair strand from breakage rather than acting on the follicle. Used sensibly they are mostly harmless, but they can irritate the scalp, and the larger cost is months spent on a remedy while the real cause of the hair fall goes unfound.</p>

<p>Oiling and kitchen remedies are part of hair care in most Indian households, often passed down through generations, and many people simply enjoy them. A weekly head massage with oil is not something that needs defending. The question here is narrower: what the published evidence says these remedies do for hair fall, where they can cause problems, and when it is time to look for a diagnosis instead.</p>

<h2 id="rosemary-oil-and-minoxidil">What the rosemary oil study actually showed</h2>

<p>The claim that "rosemary oil works as well as minoxidil" traces back to one trial: <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25842469/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">Panahi and colleagues, published in SKINmed in 2015</a>. One hundred people with androgenetic alopecia (pattern hair loss) were randomly assigned to rosemary oil or to 2% minoxidil, 50 in each group, for six months.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>At three months,</strong> hair counts had not changed significantly in either group.</li>
  <li><strong>At six months,</strong> hair counts had risen significantly in both groups, and the difference between the groups was not statistically significant.</li>
  <li><strong>Scalp itching</strong> occurred in both groups and was more common with minoxidil.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Its limits</h3>

<p>That is a genuine finding, and it is also a long way from proof.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>It is one small trial.</strong> A hundred participants across two groups is modest, and it has not been confirmed by larger, independent trials.</li>
  <li><strong>There was no placebo group.</strong> Without one, there is no way to know how much of the rise in hair counts would have happened anyway, or simply from people taking more care of their hair during a study.</li>
  <li><strong>The comparison was with 2% minoxidil,</strong> the lower of the strengths commonly used, and over only six months.</li>
  <li><strong>Not finding a difference is not the same as showing two treatments are equal.</strong> A small trial can easily miss a real difference.</li>
  <li><strong>Products vary.</strong> Rosemary oils, infusions and "rosemary water" sold today differ widely in what they contain and how concentrated they are, and there is no way to know whether a given bottle resembles what was tested.</li>
</ul>

<p>Laboratory and animal studies suggest plausible mechanisms, and reviews published since have called for more research in people. The fair summary is that rosemary oil might help some people with pattern hair loss a little, and that this has not been established. Minoxidil, by contrast, has decades of trials behind it, along with side effects and a prescription requirement that deserve a proper conversation; <a href="/blog/minoxidil-and-finasteride-in-india">minoxidil and finasteride in India</a> sets those out.</p>

<h2 id="onion-juice">Onion juice: one small study, in a different condition</h2>

<p>The evidence for onion juice is even thinner. It rests on <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12126069/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">a 2002 study by Sharquie and Al-Obaidi in the Journal of Dermatology</a>, in which 23 people with patchy alopecia areata applied crude onion juice twice daily for two months and 15 applied tap water. Most of the onion juice group showed some regrowth, compared with a small minority of the tap water group.</p>

<p>Three things limit what that means for anyone reading this with hair fall:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Alopecia areata is a different condition</strong> from shedding or pattern hair loss. It is autoimmune, and patches often regrow on their own, which makes small, short studies hard to interpret.</li>
  <li><strong>The study was tiny,</strong> and onion juice is impossible to mistake for tap water, so participants knew which treatment they had.</li>
  <li><strong>It has not been confirmed</strong> by larger, well-controlled trials in the two decades since.</li>
</ul>

<p>Explanations involving the sulphur compounds in onion remain theoretical. Onion juice also irritates some scalps, and its smell is hard to wash out.</p>

<h2 id="coconut-oil-and-oiling">Coconut oil, oiling and dandruff</h2>

<h3>What coconut oil does to the hair strand</h3>

<p>The most quoted study on hair oil is a laboratory one: <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12715094/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">Rele and Mohile, in the Journal of Cosmetic Science in 2003</a>. Testing hair fibres rather than people, it found that coconut oil reduced protein loss from both undamaged and damaged hair when applied before or after washing, while mineral oil and sunflower oil did not. The authors linked this to coconut oil's main fatty acid, which is small and straight enough to penetrate the hair shaft. The researchers worked for a hair-oil manufacturer, which is worth knowing, though the finding is widely cited.</p>

<p>Less protein loss means a stronger strand that is less likely to snap, which matters for breakage, frizz and split ends. It does not reach the follicle, so it does not change how many hairs shed from the root or slow pattern hair loss. Broken pieces have no root; a shed hair usually has a small white bulb at one end.</p>

<h3>Head massage</h3>

<p>A very small study of nine men who used a massage device on the scalp for four minutes a day over six months reported slightly thicker hairs at the end. It did not involve oil, and it is far too small to say that massage treats hair loss. A gentle champi is pleasant and unlikely to do harm; vigorous rubbing with the fingertips can break hairs.</p>

<h3>Oiling and dandruff</h3>

<p>The scalp is where oiling can backfire. Malassezia, the yeast involved in dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis, depends on fats to grow. In laboratory work, oleic acid, a fatty acid found in scalp oil and in many plant oils, triggered dandruff-like flaking in people prone to dandruff but not in others. There are no good clinical trials of hair oils in dandruff, but that mechanism, and what dermatologists see in practice, is why a heavy coat of oil on a flaking, itchy scalp often makes it worse. Keep oil to the lengths until the scalp is under control; <a href="/blog/can-dandruff-cause-hair-fall">whether dandruff causes hair fall</a> covers what does control it.</p>

<h3>Other traditional remedies</h3>

<p>Amla, bhringraj, fenugreek and curry leaves have long histories of use. Most of the research behind them is laboratory or animal work, with little testing in people with hair loss. Unless a reaction occurs, they are unlikely to cause harm in sensible use; the evidence simply does not show that they change hair fall.</p>

<h2 id="irritation-and-patch-testing">Irritation, allergy and how to patch-test a remedy</h2>

<p>Natural does not mean gentle. Raw onion, garlic, lemon juice and undiluted essential oils can all irritate skin, causing stinging, redness or a burning feeling soon after application. A strong irritant can blister. Allergic contact dermatitis is different: it usually appears a day or two after contact, itches, and can spread beyond the area where the product was applied. <a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/allergic-contact-dermatitis-to-essential-oils" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">DermNet's page on allergy to essential oils</a> notes that irritation and allergy can occur together, especially with oils used in high concentration. An allergy can also develop to something that has been used for months without trouble.</p>

<p>A simple test before using a new remedy on the whole scalp is a sensible precaution:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Prepare a small amount exactly as you intend to use it, including any dilution.</li>
  <li>Apply it to a small patch of skin on the inner forearm or behind the ear.</li>
  <li>Leave it, and watch the area for 48 hours. Reactions can take up to about three days to appear.</li>
  <li>If there is redness, itching, swelling, blistering or oozing, do not use it.</li>
</ol>

<p>A clear result lowers the risk; it does not rule out a reaction later, and it is not the same as formal patch testing arranged by a doctor. Never apply an essential oil undiluted, and do not put remedies on broken, inflamed or recently treated skin, including a scalp healing after PRP, microneedling or a transplant. If a reaction starts, rinse thoroughly with water and a mild shampoo and stop the product. Swelling of the face, lips or throat, or difficulty breathing, needs emergency care.</p>

<h2 id="why-remedies-can-delay-diagnosis">Why remedies can delay a diagnosis</h2>

<p>Hair fall is a symptom, and its causes need quite different handling. Some settle on their own, some need a blood test to find, and some keep progressing until they are treated. That is where home remedies cause their real harm: not usually through the remedy itself, but through the months spent waiting for it to work.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Shedding after an illness or a stressful period</strong> usually settles over months whatever is applied, so whichever remedy was in use at the time gets the credit. That is how many remedies earn their reputation.</li>
  <li><strong>Iron deficiency or a thyroid disorder</strong> will not respond to anything put on the scalp. <a href="/blog/blood-tests-for-hair-fall">Which blood tests matter for hair fall</a> explains how these are found.</li>
  <li><strong>Pattern hair loss</strong> progresses steadily. Each year spent on an unproven remedy is a year of follicles miniaturising. <a href="/blog/telogen-effluvium-vs-pattern-hair-loss">How shedding and pattern thinning are told apart</a> covers the difference.</li>
  <li><strong>Alopecia areata and scalp conditions</strong> are diagnosed by examination and treated in their own ways.</li>
</ul>

<p>If a remedy is part of your routine and you like it, say so at an appointment. It can usually continue alongside treatment, as long as it is not irritating the scalp.</p>

