<h2 id="when-to-see-a-doctor">When to see a doctor</h2>

<p>See a doctor about your hair if:</p>

<ul>
  <li>shedding has lasted more than about three months, or is getting heavier;</li>
  <li>the parting is widening, the hairline is moving back or the crown is thinning;</li>
  <li>there are bald patches, broken hairs, scaling, redness or soreness on the scalp;</li>
  <li>a remedy caused a rash, blistering, oozing or swelling;</li>
  <li>hair fall comes with tiredness, weight change, irregular periods or other new symptoms.</li>
</ul>

<p>None of these is needed as a reason to book. The <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/hair-loss/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">NHS guidance on hair loss</a> puts it simply: see a doctor if you are worried about your hair loss.</p>

